{
  "abstract": "Contextual token matching recovers formal rule support on CATT, but retaining a simpler representation did not reduce complete-forward extraction time. We measure two reductions with different guarantees: stopping at an already fixed residual boundary preserves its features exactly, while retaining the first complete passage per source group approximates the original group-max score. On the exposed seed 1001 targets and inherited 20 evaluation facts, the reduced index reaches 100% Recall@10 on original Rule Routings and Function Bindings with distractors, and 96.25% on changed-wording Rule Routings. Its changed-RR difference from full group-max is +1.25 percentage points [−3.75,+7.5], passing the practical point gate without establishing statistical noninferiority. Three direct paired repetitions reduce loaded-model batch medians from 261.236 to 34.198 seconds on RR and 347.899 to 23.069 seconds on FB, with exact reference scores and complete rankings in both arms. Useful source-vector bytes fall to 10.19%/10.33%, while peak allocated GPU memory remains 92.0%/85.9%; shape-preserving scratch is fully charged. Sensitivity bounds the approximation: eight of ten aligned representative choices pass all gates, independent uniform choices have exact expected changed-RR recall 94.02%, and a source-only coverage selector provides no improvement or certified query margins. All representatives teach the same observed cases, so semantic redundancy does not imply score interchangeability. Strong lexical and disabled-model controls, failed attempts, imperfect target behavior and the RR width correction remain explicit. The contribution is a reproducible quality–cost operating point, not a new matching operator or historical-influence estimate.",
